{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1468013-difficulty-r\u00e9duction-for-gifts","id":1468013,"title":"Difficulty r\u00e9duction for gifts","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-11-16T09:33:07-08:00","id":1468013,"title":"Difficulty r\u00e9duction for gifts","content":"Some gifts grants the garou the ability to reduce the difficulty of some action. Is that reduction working for gifts activation rolls?<br /> And with the good combination of gifts some action may fall below difficulty 3, could we in that case tell that there is no need to roll and that the entire pool are success? Like the action become so easy that the character cannot fail in any way","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-16T10:35:05-08:00","id":1468021,"title":"","content":"I believe that reduced difficulty does apply to Gifts as well as mundane actions. If people know better information, I'd love to hear it.<br /> <br /> The standard rule in the game is that if your dice pool equals to the difficulty level, then you can choose not to roll and gain 1 success (and 1 success only). So if their dice pool is at least 3, and the character only needs 1 success, then yeah, it can happen automatically. However, if the Gift (or any other action) is something that requires more than one success, or is done better the more successes you have, then the character should still roll.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-16T14:16:39-08:00","id":1468049,"title":"","content":"Some gift are standart roll (Attribute + Ability) vs the permanent willpower of the target, so if a gift reduce the difficulty of an attribute or an ability the willpower of the target will count as -1 or -2? That seem strange to me \ud83e\udd28<br /> <br /> I know the standart rule of the book about the automatic success but here's a case:<br /> A strong wyrm creature with 14 soaking point has been knocked out. The players want to finish him, so as the creature is stunned on the floor there is no need to roll to hit.... but a crinos with 9 in strength would roll only 11 dice in a claws attack to hit the creature ? While make an classic attack roll would give him 3/5 bonus dice to roll the damage?<br /> 1 automatic success on a difficulty 5/6/7/8 if the player has enough dice in his pool, i understand. But only 1 automatic success on a difficulty under 3 or maybe 2 with the help of some gift is absolutly unfair in my head, but maybe it's just me<br /> <br /> Another exemple: a bite attach is difficulty 5, hit in the back is -2 so the bite is difficulty 3. Now with a gift that reduce again the difficulty, the roll difficulty fall at 2 or even 1.... in that case the roll is useless because even the 1 is count as a success in that case, so all dices of the pool are success. And it should count as only 1 automatic success?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26839,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","user":"Cain Loup-Noir","date":". 11-16-2021, 05:21 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-17T22:18:18-08:00","id":1468231,"title":"","content":"IIRC, 2 is the bottom limit of difficulties. There's almost always a rule on a bottom limit somewhere in the book, general or specific to each kind of effect that reduces dif.<br /> <br /> But that aside, the thing here is when you have a roll that is important and the number of successes counts. I had my fair share of such rolls, where the difficulty is low, but each success would have an impact.<br /> <br /> Your case seems to be just excessive rolls, though. No action without dramatic consequence should ever be rolled IMHO. Rolls aren't a result of declaring an action, they're a tool for conflict resolution. If there's no conflict, whys there a roll? If you already decided their foe was brought down, just close the scene.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-18T02:56:05-08:00","id":1468249,"title":"","content":"Then in that case why does the book tell that in some situation an attack could touch without roll, allowing a direct damage roll? Like an ennemy completely immobilized on the ground for a reason or another","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-18T03:01:14-08:00","id":1468251,"title":"","content":"Plus an ennemy stunned could wake up from the pain of an injury, so a roll is usefull to know if the ennemy is definitly killed or back in combat, especially if the monster is powerfull and could get back on it's feets to fight a injured pack","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-18T08:39:39-08:00","id":1468276,"title":"","content":"The book let's you make the damage roll without an attack roll because the scene may still have a number of complications that make it relevant. Like the enemy that can get back on their feet.<br /> <br /> Yet, this same example is also one where number of successes is key: attack success affects damage pool. If you need this bonus, roll for it. You're trying to get a precise strike instead of just a strike, in a limited time scale (a turn) instead of taking your time.<br /> <br /> So, there's drama. If there's drama, there's no reason to use average results and call it a day, rolling matters. If you don't think that rolling matters, then you already decided that even the theoretical possibility of this enemy getting up is a foregone option and you can just end the scene.<br /> <br /> It's the difference between the player choosing a standard success and the ST choosing to not ask a roll in the first place. An ST don't have to ask every roll, or any roll for that matter. The ST may decide the outcome in any number of ways without a roll.<br /> <br /> As a rule of thumb, if you really have a situation where, as an ST, you want to know the effectiveness of an action without asking for a roll, then sure you can simply run the average of the pool and call it a day, but that's not an automatic success rule, instead it is the ST choosing to not ask for a roll because it adds nothing to the drama.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-18T10:23:29-08:00","id":1468280,"title":"","content":"Then we can cap the difficulty of some actions, attack an ennemy in the back need a roll but the difficulty cannot fall below 3. However some roll make the difficulty get above 10, i think about some gift that cap their difficulty on the ennemy stamina which can get for certain fomori above 10. In that case we can cap the difficulty at 10 or tell the player that the action is impossible","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-18T11:55:35-08:00","id":1468290,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cain Loup-Noir</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1468013-difficulty-r\u00e9duction-for-gifts?p=1468280#post1468280\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Then we can cap the difficulty of some actions, attack an ennemy in the back need a roll but the difficulty cannot fall below 3. However some roll make the difficulty get above 10, i think about some gift that cap their difficulty on the ennemy stamina which can get for certain fomori above 10. In that case we can cap the difficulty at 10 or tell the player that the action is impossible</div> </div> </div> </div>For 10+ difficulty I personally like to apply the Mage rule. If the action is indeed still possible, cap the difficulty at 9 and demand an extra success for each point it would have above 9. So instead of dif 10, you have dif 9 but the first success is &quot;lost&quot;, and so on. You can add that those &quot;lost&quot; successes don't count towards avoiding a botch if you prefer.<br /> <br /> In the same vein, you can decide that below 3 the character gets extra successes if the roll itself is successful (zero successes in the roll alone are still a failure). You can theoretically cap at 2, but I agree that 3 works better. This option is untested, though, try it by your own risk if you want.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-18T14:16:22-08:00","id":1468300,"title":"","content":"Or, simple idea to replace extra success below difficulty 3, a player that still want to roll do it without botch the 1 do not delete any success. If a Crinos bites an enemy that he holds with one hand above the ground and that enemy is blinded and disarmed and the player wants to roll the dice to measure the effectiveness of his bite, the botch is deactivated and the roll is made normally with difficulty 3.<br /> Same for an unconscious wyrm creature with more soaking point than the player has strength (like a thunderwyrm stunned by a huge stone fallen from a cliff thanks to the action of a player to save his pack, They would take advantage of the creature's unconsciousness to hit it with full power to pierce its carapace and seriously wound it))","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26839,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","user":"Cain Loup-Noir","date":". 11-18-2021, 05:18 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}